Id: stor008w
Severity: WARN
Category: filesystem
Check: check_storage
Fix: Blacklist usb-storage and uas in /etc/modprobe.d, or set usbcore authorized_default=0; then rebuild the initramfs.
References: modprobe.d(5)
Controls: NIST MP-7; ISO 7.10

The policy is to allow no USB storage (Tiger_Storage_NoUSB=Y in
tigerrc), but neither driver is blocked in /etc/modprobe.d (both
usb-storage and uas must be: USB3 sticks use uas when it loads),
and new devices are not left unauthorized either
(usbcore authorized_default), so a USB stick plugged in is mounted
and data can be copied off or on. The same id reports a storage
device already connected.

To fix it, add to a file in /etc/modprobe.d:

    install usb-storage /bin/false
    blacklist usb-storage
    install uas /bin/false
    blacklist uas

then rebuild the initramfs (update-initramfs -u, or dracut -f). If USB
storage is wanted, set Tiger_Storage_NoUSB=N.
